    "body": "## P0 financial correctness\n\nBoth Stripe refund restoration paths advanced local authoritative state to `walletRestored=true` without checking whether the idempotent `STRIPE_REFUND_RESTORE` ledger mutation was actually applied.\n\nThat could make a rejected/canceled refund look financially restored while the wallet ledger did not contain the restoring credit.\n\n## Fix\n\n- require `creditRestoringOperation(...)` to return `true` before marking a refund request wallet-restored;\n- fail closed with a conflict when the restoring ledger mutation was not newly applied;\n- cover both the synchronous provider-result path and signed Stripe refund webhook settlement path;\n- preserve existing restore/reserve operation keys and source-of-funds restoration semantics.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n- direct provider rejection no longer marks `walletRestored` when the restore operation reports `false`;\n- webhook-driven failed refund no longer marks `walletRestored` when the restore operation reports `false`;\n- successful webhook restoration explicitly proves the positive ledger result before advancing state.\n\n## Merge gate\n\nBranch was cut from `master` `6038f456b180d18bde793404cef24b44215dc3c6` after PR #211 was merged and its post-merge workflows completed successfully. Do not merge unless all relevant checks are green on the exact current PR head, review threads are clear, and `master` is revalidated immediately before merge. If `master` moves, integrate it safely and rerun exact-head checks. Merge with a merge commit only.",
